    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate originated not as a dynastic succession or military coup but as a religious reform movement of extraordinary ideological intensity, born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morocco">Morocco</a> in 1121 CE. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Muhammad ibn Tumart</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> theologian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masmuda">Masmuda</a> tribe who had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andalusia">Andalusia</a> and returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a> preaching strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tawhid">Tawhid</a> — the absolute unity of God — denouncing the reigning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almoravid-empire">Almoravid dynasty</a> as impious anthropomorphists who gave God physical attributes and tolerated moral laxity. Towards the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramadan">Ramadan</a> in 1121, after a particularly moving sermon, Ibn Tumart revealed himself as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahdi">Mahdi</a> — the divinely guided redeemer — and established his base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tinmel">Tinmel</a> in the Atlas Mountains. The movement's early years were marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> skirmishing against the Almoravids rather than conventional warfare, and a catastrophic defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-al-buhayra">Battle of al-Buhayra</a> in 1130 killed Ibn Tumart himself. His chief disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> concealed the founder's death for two years to prevent disintegration, eventually emerging to claim leadership and being proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliph</a> around 1133. Abd al-Mu'min — rather than Ibn Tumart — was the true architect of the Almohad state. Over a thirty-year reign, he systematically overwhelmed the Almoravids, captured their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1147, formally declared himself Caliph — the first such title claimed in the western Islamic world since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> — and completed the conquest of the entire Maghreb from Morocco to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> by 1159–1160. Simultaneously, Almohad forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, beginning the absorption of the last independent Muslim principalities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> under Abd al-Mu'min's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate governed one of the most economically diverse empires of the medieval world, spanning the agricultural plains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a>, the pastoral Saharan fringe, and the sophisticated urban civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> with its dense networks of cities, irrigated agriculture, and artisan industries. The North African agricultural base produced wheat, barley, and livestock across the Tell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghrebi</a> plains, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> routes provided revenues from the empire's southern periphery. The Andalusian economy was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a>'s most productive component: the irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huerta">huertas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#murcia">Murcia</a> generated agricultural surpluses; the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a>, Seville, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a> supported sophisticated craft industries in textiles, metalwork, and ceramics; and the maritime trade of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> connected Almohad ports to Italian, Byzantine, and Egyptian commercial networks. Seville served as the caliphate's Iberian capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#giralda">Giralda</a> minaret — begun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-mosque-of-seville">Seville's Great Mosque</a> under his patronage — stands as an architectural monument to the caliphate's peak prosperity. The Almohad administrative system imposed significant fiscal demands on subject populations, provoking periodic resistance, and the dynasty's religious rigidity — imposing conversion or exile on Jewish and Christian communities that had coexisted relatively peacefully under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> — disrupted the intellectual and commercial networks that had made Andalusian cities the most cosmopolitan in the western Mediterranean. The empire's financial resources were severely strained by the cost of simultaneously maintaining forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, a structural burden that contributed materially to its eventual fragmentation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad military machine was built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masmuda">Masmuda</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> tribal confederation that had coalesced around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>'s religious movement, supplemented by Saharan cavalry, Andalusian contingents, and later Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> transformed this tribal base into a disciplined force through systematic organisation and the ideological motivation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jihad">jihad</a>, creating an army capable of the sustained campaigning necessary to conquer both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a>'s military reach its zenith under Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaqub-al-mansur">Yaqub al-Mansur</a> (r. 1184–1199), who led the empire's most celebrated military achievement: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> on 18 July 1195, where his forces crushed the Castilian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>, inflicting catastrophic losses. The victory earned al-Mansur his honorific — "Made Victorious by God" — and his forces subsequently devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#extremadura">Extremadura</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tagus">Tagus</a> valley, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, raiding to the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a> in the deepest penetration of Christian territory by Muslim forces for generations. Al-Mansur's death in 1199 and the succession of the militarily passive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a> allowed the Christian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> to consolidate and form a coalition under papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">Crusade</a> declaration, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> on 16 July 1212. The combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, and Portugal shattered the Almohad force, al-Nasir fled the field, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> the following year reportedly in despair. This defeat broke the military mystique that had been the primary source of Almohad political cohesion, triggering the accelerating fragmentation that would extinguish the caliphate within sixty years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad governance system was characterised by a fundamental tension between its founding ideology and the practical demands of administering a vast, diverse empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> had conceived the movement as a theocratic enterprise in which religious correctness — as defined by himself as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahdi">Mahdi</a> — justified political authority, creating a governance structure in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>'s legitimacy derived from his role as the defender and enforcer of true Islamic doctrine rather than from dynastic genealogy alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> institutionalised this by establishing a governing hierarchy of Almohad "believers" — drawn exclusively from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masmuda">Masmuda</a> and other Almohad tribal confederates — who constituted a ruling class distinct from the general Muslim population. The caliphal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> maintained a sophisticated administrative apparatus influenced by the Andalusian bureaucratic tradition, and the capital was adorned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#koutoubia-mosque">Koutoubia Mosque</a> (whose minaret remains one of the finest examples of Almohad architecture), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rabat">Rabat</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kasbah-of-the-udayas">Kasbah of the Udayas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaqub-al-mansur">Yaqub al-Mansur</a>'s court patronised the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#averroes">Averroes</a> (Ibn Rushd) and the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maimonides">Maimonides</a> briefly before his expulsion, making the caliphate a centre of the highest medieval intellectual culture. The structural weakness of the governance system was its ideological dependency: authority flowed from the claim that the caliph governed as the legitimate successor of the Mahdi Ibn Tumart. When Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#idris-i-al-mamun">Idris I al-Mamun</a> publicly renounced Ibn Tumart's Mahdi status in 1229 — trading ideological coherence for Castilian military support — he simultaneously demolished the entire theological foundation on which Almohad governance rested, accelerating the disintegration that followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate's historical impact was disproportionately concentrated in two domains: military and intellectual. Militarily, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a> represented the last serious Islamic offensive against Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> (1195) demonstrated that a unified Muslim power could still threaten the entire Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#reconquista">Reconquista</a> project. The subsequent defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> (1212) marked the decisive turning point after which Muslim political power in Iberia entered terminal decline, eventually producing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fall-of-cordoba">fall of Córdoba</a> (1236), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> (1248), and — two centuries later — <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a> (1492). Intellectually, the Almohad court, particularly under al-Mansur, was one of the most important centres of philosophical and scientific activity in the medieval world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#averroes">Averroes</a> (Ibn Rushd), working under Almohad patronage, produced his commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aristotle">Aristotle</a> that would transform European scholastic philosophy when transmitted through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> translation, influencing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and the entire trajectory of medieval Western thought. The Almohad unification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a> — however briefly — created an administrative and commercial framework that influenced the subsequent Hafsid, Zayyanid, and Marinid successor states. Architecturally, the Almohad contribution to the western Islamic world was lasting: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#koutoubia-mosque">Koutoubia Mosque</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rabat">Rabat</a> fortifications, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#giralda">Giralda</a> of Seville are among the finest surviving monuments of medieval Islamic architecture, and the Giralda's conversion into a cathedral bell tower following the Christian reconquest of Seville made it a permanent fixture of that city's skyline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate's decline began with a single catastrophic military defeat and accelerated through a sequence of institutional failures that progressively stripped the empire of its territorial, ideological, and political coherence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212 broke not merely the Almohad army but the military prestige that had been the primary source of caliphal authority: a dynasty whose legitimacy rested on divinely guaranteed victory could not absorb a crushing battlefield defeat without existential damage to its governing ideology. The succession crisis that followed the weak reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yusuf-ii">Yusuf II</a> (1214–1224) produced multiple simultaneous caliphal claimants, provincial governors who defected to Christian kingdoms in exchange for local autonomy, and a rapid territorial unravelling. The double catastrophe of 1229 — the Hafsid declaration of independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#idris-i-al-mamun">al-Mamun</a>'s renunciation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahdi">Mahdi</a> status in exchange for Castilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> — simultaneously removed the eastern third of the African domain and demolished the theological foundation of the entire enterprise. The Zayyanid seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> around 1236 carved out the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fall-of-cordoba">fall of Córdoba</a> (1236) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> (1248) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> ended centuries of Muslim rule in the cities most closely associated with Andalusian civilisation. By 1269, the last Almohad Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#idris-al-wathiq">Idris al-Wathiq</a> controlled only the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, where he was murdered by a slave. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> immediately seized the capital, extinguishing the dynasty that had once ruled from the Atlantic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tagus">Tagus</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Almohad Caliphate's enduring significance lies in its role as both the last great unifier of the western Islamic world and, through the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#averroes">Averroes</a> produced under its patronage, an inadvertent catalyst of the European intellectual revolution that would eventually transform the global balance of power.</em></p>

